STYLE CORE (ASHLAR art direction, mandatory): moderately stylized hand-painted dark fantasy in the spirit of Diablo Immortal (the Diablo III / Diablo Immortal family look). NOT photoreal, NOT Diablo IV realism, NOT cartoon, chibi, anime, cel-shaded or toy-like. Proportions follow real anatomy; exaggeration goes only to the identity features (heroes: hands, feet and class props slightly oversized; monsters: their signature features pushed further). Strong readable silhouette, clear value grouping, clean colour blocking: a dominant colour, a secondary colour and a small accent, plus neutrals. Surfaces are SIMPLE: broad painted planes with soft gradients and a few crisp highlights; detail comes from big shapes (large torn edges, plates, straps, bones, cracks, patches), never from texture. No micro texture: no pores, fabric weave, leather grain, scratches, stains, speckle noise, fine grunge or photoreal PBR. Low detail never means flat: keep clear 3D form shading with a lit side, a shadow side, big soft fold shadows and darker occlusion in deep folds. No black cartoon outlines.

T-POSE RULES: keep the concept's design, build and smooth painted low-detail surface; only the pose changes. Keep the concept's hand-painted form shading: every big fold and cloth layer has a lit plane and a large dark shadow plane (deep shadow in fold recesses, under capes, hoods, collars, belts and sashes, inside splits); roughly a third of each cloth surface in shadow; never one evenly lit flat colour. One neutral white light from above and slightly from the camera side, the SAME in every view; no side light, no rim light, no coloured light, no glow or bloom. Cloth is one plain clean shape (no ragged fringe, no loose strips). Hands empty; hand-held weapons and props are built separately. Perfectly flat neutral mid-grey background; no text, no human silhouette.

CONTENT: no crosses, no real saints, prayers or religious symbols; ASHLAR's church sign is the Wedge (a keystone shape), only where the brief asks for it. No gore emphasis and no blood splatter (Act III blood accents are small painted stains only); heroes show no wounds and no blood. Adults only; no nudity, no anatomical detail, no sexualised poses; no likeness of any real person. Never stage hanged bodies.

Use case: character turnaround for image-to-3D (Tripo multi-view input).
REFERENCE IMAGES (attached): (1) the concept image of the creature (it may be a lineup with an H silhouette and several views): identity reference for the DESIGN only (colours, materials, face, hat/armour, parts worn on the body, proportions). Keep the same design. Do not copy the lineup layout, the H silhouette, any text, or the concept's pose. 
ASHLAR monster P01 The Soothed, variant A: a gentle sleep-walking adult woman (a victim, calm and tender, not a horror), a tall narrow column silhouette, slim natural build, smooth painted low-detail surface. Head = ONE concrete shape in every view: a plain soft cream cloth cap (a rounded bonnet) covering the top and back of the head, gathered into one small soft knot at the back of the head; long straight brown hair #5A4636 comes out under the cap and falls behind the shoulders and down the back as ONE thick smooth mass, plus two thick locks in front of the shoulders, no loose strands. Face: calm, eyes closed as two smooth lids, a faint gentle smile; a small red stitch mark #B3262A over the mouth (one curved line with three short ticks, a painted mark only: no blood, no wound). HEAD LEVEL AND UPRIGHT, chin level, face looking straight ahead: the concept tilts her head onto one shoulder, but in this T-pose the head is NOT tilted. A long straight loose nightgown of cream linen #E8E1D0 (PRIMARY) from the shoulders to mid-shin, its hem cut into a few big shallow jagged points, a short vertical slit at the neckline, wide bell sleeves to the mid-forearm whose lower edge hangs only a little below the arm; large soft brown mud stains #8A7350 on the lower third of the gown and on the shins. A faded slate-blue shawl #5F6B7A (SECONDARY) as ONE thick soft piece lying over both shoulders, the tops of the upper arms and the upper back: in front its two ends hang straight down beside the chest from the armpits to about half the height; at the back it hangs as one big triangle with a few large jagged points, its lowest point at the middle of the back at about three quarters of the height; it does not hang down from the arms like wings and does not join the arms to the body. Barefoot: plain bare feet and shins, pale skin. Hand-painted form shading with big soft fold shadows down the gown, under the shawl and inside the sleeves.
Canvas: one landscape 3:2 image (1536x1024 or larger) with TWO full-body views of the same creature side by side: left FRONT view, right BACK view (the true back, not a mirrored front).
POSE = STRICT T-POSE (the most important requirement): the creature stands on both feet, torso nearly upright (only a very slight forward lean, NOT hunched, NOT crouched), knees almost straight, feet flat on the floor shoulder-width apart pointing forward. BOTH ARMS are stretched fully STRAIGHT OUT TO THE SIDES, perfectly HORIZONTAL at shoulder height, forming one straight horizontal line with the shoulders (a capital letter T), elbows straight, wrists straight, palms facing down, fingers open and slightly spread. The arms are clearly separated from the torso and from anything worn on the back, and do not hang down or bend. Head level, looking straight ahead, not hidden by anything worn on the back. If the arms are long the arm span is wider than the body is tall: keep the whole span inside the frame with at least 5 percent margin; the figure may be about 45 to 50 percent of the image height.
HUMANOID BODY RULES (critical; this image is the reference for a skeleton, so it must read like a clean neutral mannequin pose of THIS character): About 7 heads tall (cap top to soles 0.97 H), slim, narrow shoulders about 0.22 of the height wide; the arm span (fingertip to fingertip) about equal to the height (0.99 in the concept's front view); human plantigrade legs, crotch hidden by the gown at about half the height, knees hidden at about 0.27; slender neck mostly hidden by the hair; slender normal hands and bare feet; the gown hides the legs down to mid-shin (hem at about 0.14 of the height above the soles).
- Perfectly upright and symmetric: weight evenly on both legs, NO contrapposto, NO hip tilt, NO stepping forward, NO crossed leg, no knee bent for a step or a crouch. BOTH LEGS IDENTICAL and in the neutral standing position: human legs are straight from hip to ankle with straight knees; if the concept's legs are digitigrade, hoofed or bird-like, keep that exact leg anatomy including its hock bend (never turn them into human legs) and only make both legs identical and vertical. Feet flat on the floor and parallel, pointing straight forward, the centres of the two feet about 0.15 of the body height apart (about one shoulder width, NOT a wide stance). Hips level, spine straight, head level, chin level.
- LEVEL SYMMETRIC SHOULDERS: both shoulders at exactly the same height, forming one straight horizontal line with both arms. No shrugged, sloped or drooping shoulder. A pauldron, fur mantle, strap or cape on one shoulder sits on the shoulder and does not lift or drop it. The arms leave the body AT shoulder height, never from below it.
- ARMS: each arm goes from the shoulder in one perfectly horizontal straight line to the fingertips (not drooping towards the hands, not rising). Each whole arm (shoulder to fingertip) is about 0.44 of the body height, so the arm span about equals the body height; upper arm and forearm about the same length; elbow and wrist straight; hand in line with the forearm, open and flat, palm down, fingers straight and slightly apart. Both arms identical in length and height.
- LEGS AND SKIRTS: for human legs the crotch is about half the body height and the knee about 27 percent of the height above the soles (other leg types: as PROPORTIONS says). A long skirt, robe or coat hangs straight down and symmetric, hiding the legs; a slit or opening does not open because a leg is stepping out. Boots stand flat and side by side.
- The FRONT and BACK views have the same head size, shoulder height, hip height, knee height and floor line.
Camera: eye level, orthographic look, no perspective, no tilt, no 3/4 angle.
Both views at the same scale on the same floor line, whole body visible with margin, no overlap.
Fidelity: keep the concept's body build exactly (a thin lean creature stays thin and lean: NO added muscle definition, NO abs or six-pack, NO claws, NO teeth, NO extra anatomy or ornaments the concept does not show), the same face and ears, the same smooth painted surface. Only the pose changes.
Low detail: broad clean material masses, few small patterns, no fine wear, grain or noise. Keep cloth a simple plain clean shape (no ragged fringe, no loose strips). Low detail does NOT mean flat: keep the big folds and their big light and shadow shapes.
Light and shading (keep the concept's volume): hand-painted form shading with the same value contrast as the concept. Every big fold and every cloth layer reads as a solid volume with a lit plane and a large dark shadow plane: deep shadow in the fold recesses, under the cape, hood, collar, belt and sash, inside splits and openings, on the inner and lower faces of panels; lit tops on the ridges of the folds; roughly a third of each cloth surface sits in shadow, as in the concept, never one evenly lit flat colour with only thin fold lines. Leather, metal, skin and body get the same form shading. The light comes from above and slightly from the camera side and is the SAME in every view, so the left and right halves of the body are lit alike (no side light that leaves one half dark). Neutral white light: no rim light, no coloured light, no glow or bloom, no shadow cast on the background; only a faint contact shadow under the feet.
Background: perfectly flat neutral mid-grey.
Nothing is held (she has no weapon or prop); leave out the concept's head tilt and its reaching arm pose. Hands empty and open. No other loose props.
Asymmetric details stay on the same body side in every view: Nearly symmetric. The shawl is symmetric with both front ends the same length; the hair mass is centred on the back; the cap knot is centred at the back of the head; the stitch mark is centred on the mouth. The head is level, not tilted to either side, in every view. LEFT view: her left side toward the camera, facing screen-left; RIGHT view: her right side toward the camera, facing screen-right.
No text, labels, numbers, logo, watermark or scenery. No human silhouette.

LANDMARK CONSISTENCY: Heights are % of the figure height (0 % = top of the cap, 100 % = soles) and are the SAME in every view. Cap 0-9 %; its knot at the back of the head at 4-10 % (BACK middle; a small bump at the back of the head in LEFT and RIGHT; hidden in FRONT). Face 6-15 %. Hair: one thick mass down the back to about 38 % (BACK; in LEFT and RIGHT it lies along the back behind the shoulder down to 38 %); in FRONT only two locks in front of the shoulders to about 28 %. Shoulders at about 18 %, arms horizontal with the hands at about 20 %; the bell sleeves' lower edge at about 30 %. Shawl: over the shoulders from about 16 %; its front ends hang beside the chest down to about 48 % (FRONT at both sides of the chest; LEFT and RIGHT at the side of the torso, in front of the arm line); its back triangle ends at about 75 % at the middle of the back (BACK; in LEFT and RIGHT its back edge runs down behind the back to the same 75 %). Neckline slit 15-24 % (FRONT only). Gown hem 84-88 % all round. Mud stains 62-88 % front and back. Shins 87-96 %, feet 96-100 %. Legs under the gown: two straight legs side by side in FRONT and BACK, one leg hiding the other in LEFT and RIGHT.


